---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import InstallSequence from "../components/InstallSequence.astro";
import HeroPanel from "../components/HeroPanel.astro";
import PillarVisual from "../components/PillarVisual.astro";
import ToolTable from "../components/ToolTable.astro";
import PricingCards from "../components/PricingCards.astro";
import Proof from "../components/Proof.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import { SITE, CTA } from "../lib/site";
import { OUTCOMES, PILLARS, METRICS, INSIGHTS, FAQS, PLANS } from "../lib/content";

/**
 * The FAQ, as structured data as well as as markup.
 *
 * `FAQPage` is the one schema type on this site that can change what a result
 * *looks like* rather than only how it is filed — the questions can appear
 * expandable under the link. Google requires the answer in the schema to match
 * the answer on the page, which is why both read from `FAQS` rather than one
 * being a rewrite of the other.
 *
 * `Base` would otherwise supply `SoftwareApplication` for this page; passing an
 * array keeps it and adds these two, because the home page is still primarily a
 * page about a piece of software.
 */
/** One button style for the four "read the detail page" links below. */
const BUTTON =
  "inline-flex h-10 items-center rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface px-4 text-sm font-medium text-ink-100 transition-colors hover:border-line-strong hover:bg-surface-2";

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
    name: SITE.name,
    description: SITE.description,
    applicationCategory: "DeveloperApplication",
    operatingSystem: "Any",
    url: new URL("/", Astro.site).href,
    publisher: { "@id": `${SITE.url}/#organization` },
    offers: PLANS.map((plan) => ({
      "@type": "Offer",
      name: plan.name,
      price: plan.price.month,
      priceCurrency: "USD",
      url: SITE.signup,
    })),
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: FAQS.map((faq) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: faq.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: faq.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Base schema={schema}>
  <!-- ── Hero ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div aria-hidden="true" class="grid-fade absolute inset-0 -z-10"></div>
    <div
      aria-hidden="true"
      class="absolute -top-40 left-1/2 -z-10 h-[30rem] w-[60rem] -translate-x-1/2 rounded-full bg-cyan/[0.07] blur-3xl"
    >
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 pt-36 pb-20 sm:px-8 sm:pt-44">
      {/*
        No `xl:max-w-[34rem]` here and no `xl:min-h-[54rem]` on the container.

        Both existed to hold open a column for a panel floated to the right of
        this copy. The panel now sits below it at full width, so the copy is
        free to use the reading measure it wants and the section is free to be
        as tall as its content.
      */}
      <div class="reveal max-w-3xl">
        {/*
          What this is, and who it is for, before anything clever.

          This slot used to hold the blog promo, which meant the first words a
          cold visitor read were "We read the schemas of 4,749 MCP servers".
          That is a good hook and it is not an introduction: it never says what
          is being sold, and the headline underneath it is a question rather than
          a category. Somebody arriving from a search or a link could read the
          whole hero and still not know this is an analytics product.

          One line fixes it. It is the same sentence `site.ts` has always used to
          describe the product to search engines and to the OG card — the site
          just never said it to a human. The headline keeps doing its job, which
          is to make the category matter.

          "for the people who build them" is the audience half, and it is here
          because "MCP servers" alone does not distinguish an author from someone
          installing one. The product is for authors; the page should say so in
          the first line rather than let a reader work it out from the pricing.
        */}
        <p class="text-sm font-medium tracking-wide text-cyan">
          Analytics for MCP servers — for the people who build them
        </p>

        {/*
          A statement, not a question.

          This was "Your MCP server works. Does it work for the model?" — good
          writing and the wrong job. Three faults, in order of how much they cost:
          it asserts nothing, so the reader does the work; "work for the model"
          never says *how*, so the tension it builds has nothing behind it; and it
          takes two sentences to arrive at a setup rather than a promise.

          It is also the same fault every `h2` on this site was just rewritten to
          remove. Clever-over-clear was a tic, and the headline was not exempt
          from the fix for being the headline.

          What replaced it opens on the verb, names what you get, and lets
          "actually" carry the whole tension the old pair spent two sentences
          building — the gap between a server that runs and a server a model can
          use. The muted second half keeps the two-tone shape the layout was built
          around, so nothing below it had to move.
        */}
        <h1
          class="mt-4 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance text-ink-100 sm:text-6xl sm:leading-[1.05] xl:text-5xl xl:leading-[1.08]"
        >
          See what models
          <span class="text-ink-700">actually</span> do with your MCP server
        </h1>

        {/*
          What you get, in the first clause.

          This was forty-three words that opened on the problem — "you publish a
          server and see nothing" — followed by three more things you cannot see,
          and only reached the product in the last nine words. Every sentence in
          it was true and it was the wrong shape for the position: a reader four
          seconds into a page they did not plan to be on will not spend three
          clauses on a problem before finding out what is being sold.

          So the four things you get lead, and the problem statement moves into
          the sections below, which is where a reader who is still here has the
          patience for it. Same claims, twenty-eight words, and the first six say
          what the product does.
        */}
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-8 text-pretty text-ink-500 sm:text-xl">
          Which tools they call, which they retry, which come back empty, and what your schemas cost
          every session — from two lines inside your own server.
        </p>

        {/*
          Two actions, and the second one asks for nothing.

          This slot has now held three things, and the reasoning is worth keeping
          because each version was better than the last for the same reason.

          It began as `npm install @mcpulse/sdk`, which assumes the reader has
          already decided to install — so a visitor who had not decided had
          exactly one thing to click and it was the sign-up.

          Then it was "See pricing", which is at least a way to keep reading. But
          pricing answers "what will this cost me", and a reader four seconds in
          has an earlier question: is this thing any good, and does it apply to
          me. Sending them to a price list to find out is asking them to evaluate
          an offer before they have evaluated the product.

          The checker answers that question instead, and it is the only thing on
          this site that gives somebody something before they give us anything:
          paste a schema, get scored against 4,749 servers, in their own browser,
          no account. For a visitor who is not ready to sign up that is a better
          offer than a number — and the ones it sends away leave having seen the
          product's argument demonstrated on their own server.

          Pricing loses nothing by moving: it is in the header, and the block
          further down this page.
        */}
        <div class="mt-10 flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-center">
          <a
            href={SITE.signup}
            class="inline-flex h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-xl bg-btn px-5 text-sm font-medium text-btn-fg transition-colors hover:bg-btn-hover"
          >
            {CTA.primary}
          </a>

          <a
            href="/check"
            class="inline-flex h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-xl border border-line px-5 text-sm font-medium text-ink-300 transition-colors hover:border-line-strong hover:text-ink-100"
          >
            Try the schema checker
          </a>
        </div>

        {/*
          The reassurance, and then the requirements — in that order.

          This line was "Node 20.12+ · zero runtime dependencies · works with any
          MCP transport", which is a specification. A specification answers a
          question the reader has not asked yet; what they are weighing at this
          point is what saying yes costs them. Free for one server, no card and
          no trial is the answer, and unlike most versions of that sentence it is
          literally true — there is no trial to expire because the free tier is
          not one.

          The requirements keep their line underneath, because for this audience
          "zero runtime dependencies" is genuinely persuasive — just not before
          the price.
        */}
        <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-ink-300">
          Free forever. No card, and no trial to expire.
        </p>

        {/*
          The blog promo, demoted from above the headline to below the fold-line.

          It was the first thing on the page. It is still the strongest thing we
          have to offer somebody who is not ready to sign up — but "here is our
          research" is a second-paragraph move, not an opening line, and it was
          standing where the category belonged.
        */}
        <a
          href="/blog/reading-5000-mcp-schemas"
          class="mt-8 inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-line bg-surface-2 py-1 pr-3 pl-1 text-xs text-ink-300 transition-colors hover:border-cyan/30 hover:text-ink-100"
        >
          <span class="rounded-full bg-cyan-wash px-2 py-0.5 font-medium text-cyan">New</span>
          We read the schemas of 4,749 MCP servers →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    {/*
      The app, below the copy and fully visible.

      ── What this replaces ────────────────────────────────────────────────
      It was `absolute` at `left-[calc(50%+2.5rem)]` with a fixed `w-[68rem]`,
      deliberately cropped — the old comment argued that running off the edge
      "makes it read as a window onto something bigger". Two problems with that
      in practice.

      It was cut hard enough to lose content: at 1440px the p95 card was sliced
      mid-label and the tool-health table lost its right half, so the thing
      being shown off was half unreadable. A 1088px panel cannot fit beside a
      544px column inside a 1280px container — that arithmetic never worked, and
      widening the container to `max-w-7xl` did not change it.

      And it was `hidden xl:block`, so below 1280px there was no product on the
      page at all. Every phone and most laptops got the argument without the
      thing it was arguing about.

      Full width below the copy fixes both: nothing is cropped, and it appears
      at every breakpoint. `HeroPanel`'s own root has no fixed width, so it
      fills whatever it is given. Below `sm` the wrapper scrolls rather than
      squeezing — a dashboard compressed to 390px is not a picture of anything,
      and a scroll is honest about that.
    */}
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 pb-24 sm:px-8 sm:pb-32">
      {/*
        `overflow-x-auto` with vertical room for the glow.

        `HeroPanel` draws a soft `-inset-10 blur-3xl` wash behind itself, which
        extends 40px past the panel on every side. Inside a scroll container
        that counts toward `scrollWidth`, so the first version produced a 40px
        horizontal scrollbar at 1024, 1440 and 1920 — on a panel that fitted
        perfectly. The negative margins give the wash somewhere to sit that is
        not overflow, and the padding puts the panel back where it belongs.
      */}
      <div
        class="reveal -mx-5 overflow-x-auto overflow-y-hidden px-5 py-10 sm:-mx-10 sm:px-10"
        style="--reveal-delay: 200ms"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
        <div class="min-w-[52rem]">
          <HeroPanel />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── The four outcomes ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <!-- Its own band rather than part of the hero: every number further down the
       page is one of these four counted a different way. -->
  <section class="border-t border-line-soft">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-12 sm:px-8">
      <ul
        class="reveal grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-line bg-line sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4"
      >
        {
          OUTCOMES.map((outcome) => (
            <li class="bg-ink-950 p-5">
              <p class={`font-mono text-sm ${outcome.tone}`}>{outcome.name}</p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-ink-500">{outcome.detail}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      {/* Telling `crashed` from `tool_error` is the hardest thing the SDK does
          and this strip only names the result. The section that explains it is
          two clicks away otherwise. */}
      <p class="reveal mt-5 text-sm text-ink-700">
        <a href="/how-it-works#outcomes" class="font-medium text-cyan hover:underline">
          How a crash is told from an error return
        </a>
         — and the fifth state that rides alongside these four.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── What you actually get ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  {/*
    The screen you live in, before the page explains anything.

    The hero draws the overview — four totals and a chart, which answers "is
    anything wrong". This answers the next question, "wrong where", and it is
    the one a reader is really asking: what do I look at after I install this.

    It sits this early because everything below is an argument, and an argument
    lands better once the reader can picture the thing being argued about. It
    also carries the four cases the Insights section names further down in
    prose, so the two read as one product seen twice rather than two demos.
  */}
  <section class="border-t border-line-soft">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-24 sm:px-8">
      <div class="reveal max-w-2xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-cyan">What you get</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance text-ink-100 sm:text-4xl">
          See every tool, and where models get stuck
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-7 text-pretty text-ink-500">
          One row per tool. How often it is reached, how often the model got what it needed on the
          first try, how often it came back empty, and what it costs to answer. The two rows worth
          opening are coloured; the rest are left alone.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ToolTable class="reveal mt-12 hidden lg:block" />
      <ToolTable class="reveal mt-12 lg:hidden" density="compact" />

      {/*
        Three red cells, each linked to the post that explains it.

        This section had no way out of it, and a generic "read more" button would
        have been the wrong fix: the reader's question here is not "tell me more
        about the product", it is "why does a tool return empty and why is that
        my problem". There is an essay answering each of those exactly, and the
        cards are already named after them.

        So each card becomes the link. It also earns its keep for search: these
        three posts previously had no route in except the blog index and the
        related-post row at the foot of another post.
      */}
      <div class="reveal mt-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
        {
          [
            {
              t: "search_orders retries 2.4×",
              d: "The model asked, got something it could not use, and asked again with different arguments. Twice, on average, before moving on.",
              href: "/blog/first-call-success",
            },
            {
              t: "get_invoice returns empty 12% of the time",
              d: "An empty array is a 200. No error was recorded anywhere, and the model got nothing it could use.",
              href: "/blog/empty-results-are-failures",
            },
            {
              t: "export_report has never been called",
              d: "Registered, described, and dead — while costing 480 tokens of schema on every single session.",
              href: "/blog/dead-tools",
            },
          ].map((item, i) => (
            <a
              href={item.href}
              class="group flex flex-col rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 transition-colors hover:border-line-strong hover:bg-surface-2"
              style={`--reveal-delay: ${i * 80}ms`}
            >
              <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink-100">{item.t}</p>
              <p class="mt-2 flex-1 text-sm leading-6.5 text-ink-500">{item.d}</p>
              {/* Always visible, not revealed on hover.

                  `opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100` was the first version, and
                  it hides the only thing marking these cards as links from every
                  reader on a touchscreen — where there is no hover state and the
                  label simply never appears. Muted and always present does the
                  same job for a mouse and does not fail for a thumb. */}
              <p class="mt-4 text-sm font-medium text-ink-500 transition-colors group-hover:text-cyan">
                Why this happens →
              </p>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── How it works ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="how" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line-soft">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-24 sm:px-8">
      <div class="reveal max-w-2xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-cyan">How it works</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100 sm:text-4xl">
          It runs inside your server, not in front of it
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-7 text-ink-500">
          Nothing about your server should have to change so that you can see it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- `min-w-0` on each card because a grid item's default
           `min-width: auto` will not shrink below its content's min-content
           width — and an SVG with no width/height has an intrinsic 300px. That
           floor made every card 363px wide inside a 280px column at 320px,
           where the grid's own overflow-hidden quietly cropped the right edge.
           Padding eases off on small screens for the same reason. -->
      <div class="mt-14 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-line bg-line lg:grid-cols-3">
        {
          PILLARS.map((pillar, i) => (
            <article
              class="reveal min-w-0 bg-ink-950 p-5 sm:p-7"
              style={`--reveal-delay: ${i * 90}ms`}
            >
              <PillarVisual kind={pillar.kind} />
              <span class="mt-6 block font-mono text-xs text-ink-700">0{i + 1}</span>
              <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100">{pillar.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6.5 text-ink-500">{pillar.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      {/*
        The way through to the page that argues this properly.

        Each of these three cards is a claim compressed into forty words. The
        reader who does not believe one of them — and "we cannot see your
        arguments" is the one they should not simply believe — needs somewhere to
        go, and until now the only route was the header.
      */}
      <a href="/how-it-works" class:list={["reveal mt-8", BUTTON]}>
        Where your traffic goes, and what leaves your process →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Install ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="border-t border-line-soft">
    <InstallSequence />

    {/* The language question, answered where it is asked. The sequence above is
        TypeScript, and a reader who writes something else needs the page that
        lists all ten rather than a header link they have already scrolled past. */}
    <div class="mx-auto -mt-10 max-w-7xl px-5 pb-24 sm:px-8">
      <a href="/install" class:list={["reveal", BUTTON]}>
        All ten SDKs, and every option →
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Insights ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="border-t border-line-soft">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-24 sm:px-8">
      <div class="reveal max-w-2xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-cyan">Insights</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100 sm:text-4xl">
          It tells you what to fix, not just what happened
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-7 text-ink-500">
          A dashboard that makes you work out what to do with it has done half the job. Every
          insight names the tool, counts the thing, and says what it is costing you.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
        {
          INSIGHTS.map((insight, i) => (
            <article
              class:list={[
                "reveal rounded-2xl border p-5",
                insight.tone === "bad"
                  ? "border-bad/20 bg-bad/[0.04]"
                  : "border-warn/20 bg-warn/[0.04]",
              ]}
              style={`--reveal-delay: ${(i % 2) * 90}ms`}
            >
              <p class="font-mono text-sm text-ink-100">{insight.tool}</p>
              <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-300">{insight.text}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      {/* Four examples invite the obvious question — what makes one appear —
          and the thresholds are written down on the metrics page. */}
      <a href="/metrics#insights" class:list={["reveal mt-8", BUTTON]}>
        The rules that trigger these →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Metrics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="metrics" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line-soft">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-24 sm:px-8">
      <div class="reveal max-w-2xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-cyan">Metrics</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100 sm:text-4xl">
          Sixteen metrics, from schema cost to first-call success
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-7 text-ink-500">
          Most are counted as your calls arrive. Three need a night's worth of calls in order and
          are labelled as of yesterday, because pretending otherwise would be the first lie the
          product told you.
        </p>

        {/*
          The way through to `/metrics`, which is now this page's job.

          Metrics left the header, and before that change this section did not
          link to it at all — the page was reachable from the footer and nowhere
          else. A reader who has just read a section about sixteen metrics is the
          single most likely person to want the page that lists them, so the link
          belongs here rather than in a row of five.
        */}
        <a href="/metrics" class:list={["mt-6", BUTTON]}>
          All sixteen, and when each one updates →
        </a>
      </div>

      {/* Numbered straight through 01–16 across the three groups, so the
          count in the heading is something you can verify rather than take on
          trust. `index` walks the flattened list. */}
      <div class="mt-16 space-y-12">
        {
          METRICS.map((column, groupIndex) => {
            const offset = METRICS.slice(0, groupIndex).reduce((n, g) => n + g.items.length, 0);

            return (
              <section class="reveal">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-4 gap-y-1 border-b border-line pb-4">
                  <h3 class="flex items-center gap-2.5 text-[15px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100">
                    <span aria-hidden="true" class:list={["h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full", column.dot]} />
                    {column.group}
                  </h3>
                  <p class="text-sm text-ink-700">{column.note}</p>
                  {/* Spelled out. A bare "11" beside tiles numbered 01–16 reads
                      as another index rather than a count of the group. */}
                  <p class="ml-auto text-xs text-ink-700">
                    {column.items.length} metrics
                  </p>
                </div>

                {/* Spaced cards rather than a hairline grid sharing one
                    background. A group of 11 in three columns leaves a cell
                    empty, and with the grid's own background showing through
                    that gap rendered as a filled tile — a box with nothing in
                    it, in a section about there being sixteen of something. */}
                <ul class="mt-5 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
                  {column.items.map((item, i) => (
                    <li class="group relative overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 transition-colors hover:border-line-strong hover:bg-surface-2">
                      {/* The rule lights up on hover instead of a border moving,
                          so nothing in the grid shifts by a pixel. */}
                      <span
                        aria-hidden="true"
                        class:list={[
                          "absolute inset-y-0 left-0 w-0.5 origin-top scale-y-0 transition-transform duration-300 group-hover:scale-y-100",
                          column.dot,
                        ]}
                      />

                      <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
                        <span class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-700">
                          {String(offset + i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
                        </span>
                        <h4 class="text-[15px] leading-6 font-medium text-ink-100">{item.name}</h4>
                      </div>

                      <p class="mt-2 pl-8 text-sm leading-6 text-ink-500">{item.detail}</p>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </section>
            );
          })
        }
      </div>

      <p class="reveal mt-12 max-w-3xl border-l-2 border-warn/40 pl-5 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-500">
        Retries are the hard one, and the one worth having. Within a session, the same tool called
        twice inside 30 seconds with <em class="text-ink-100 not-italic">different</em> arguments is
        the model rewording and trying again. With the <em class="text-ink-100 not-italic">same</em>
        arguments it is pagination or polling, and it is not a retry. Get that distinction wrong and
        every well-behaved paginating tool grades as broken.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Pricing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  {/*
    On the home page, not only on `/pricing`.

    "What does it cost" is the question every visitor has and the one this page
    used to answer by sending them somewhere else. A price behind a click reads
    as a price with something to hide, and for a product whose free tier is a
    genuine offer that is the opposite of the truth.

    The cards are the component `/pricing` renders, so the two pages cannot
    disagree. What stays on `/pricing` is the part that needs room: caps,
    downgrades, who takes the payment.
  */}
  <section id="pricing" class="scroll-mt-24 border-t border-line-soft">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-24 sm:px-8">
      <div class="reveal mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-cyan">Pricing</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance text-ink-100 sm:text-4xl">
          Free for one server. $49 for a server people depend on.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-7 text-pretty text-ink-500">
          Every plan records all sixteen metrics and reaches its own data through the API and an MCP
          connector. What you are buying is how much traffic, how far back, and whether it tells you
          when something breaks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <PricingCards class="mt-14" />

      <div class="reveal mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-sm">
        <span class="text-ink-700">
          Every plan starts free. No trial to expire, no card up front.
        </span>
        <a href="/pricing" class="font-medium text-cyan hover:underline">
          Caps, downgrades and who takes the payment →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── The checker ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  {/*
